republic-function.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: republic-function.logic.r1 has "soul_has_ends ∧ soul_has_excellence ∧ cannot_fulfil_without → good_rules_well ∧ evil_rules_ill" and "good_rules_well ∧ evil_rules_ill ∧ life_end ∧ justice_excellence ∧ injustice_defect → just_lives_well ∧ unjust_lives_ill" and "just_happy ∧ unjust_miserable ∧ happiness_profitable → ¬injustice_more_profitable"; republic-function.logic.r1.2 has "justice_excellence ∧ good_rules_well ∧ life_end → just_lives_well" and "injustice_defect ∧ evil_rules_ill ∧ life_end → unjust_lives_ill" and "just_happy ∧ unjust_miserable ∧ happiness_profitable → ¬injustice_more_profitable"; they differ when only "the soul has an end which nothing else can fulfil, such as to superintend, command and deliberate", "life is to be reckoned among the ends of the soul", "the soul has an excellence", "the soul cannot fulfil her own ends when deprived of her excellence", "the good soul is a good ruler and superintendent", "an evil soul is an evil ruler and superintendent", "justice is the excellence of the soul", "the unjust man will live ill", "the just is happy", "the unjust is miserable" and "happiness and not misery is profitable" are true, which the first allows; republic-function.logic.r1.3 has "just_happy ∧ unjust_miserable ∧ happiness_profitable → ¬injustice_more_profitable"
republic-function.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: republic-function.logic.r1 takes for granted "if (the soul has an end which nothing else can fulfil, such as to superintend, command and deliberate and the soul has an excellence and the soul cannot fulfil her own ends when deprived of her excellence), then (the good soul is a good ruler and superintendent and an evil soul is an evil ruler and superintendent)"; republic-function.logic.r1.2 and republic-function.logic.r1.3 do not
republic-function.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps republic-function.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the soul has an end which nothing else can fulfil, such as to superintend, command and deliberate and the soul has an excellence and the soul cannot fulfil her own ends when deprived of her excellence), then (the good soul is a good ruler and superintendent and an evil soul is an evil ruler and superintendent)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
